#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166468 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "2e24d040-266c-5ba5-bb2b-9918c22eb48e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21596" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/040ac76a28687db1c99d3379242b726a85aa4baf269a7c6d35d9662af1d3bd78.png)" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/2ee58da2733a468406869e43f57deebc6f7924f1d1149c0ca9391ea9995d9f87.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/83596c7f3038e585eb35ffafc966810e6891b55fc4d1efb04494bdc4b3755b05.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/af5b69c599a13a707365e88f6f50939064c7b199a1d8231b32a1c88e425440fa.png)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/833ce06c0c6d5f9beea87a84ef1b286748472c312987e837ca6f47f60225b1fa.png)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/fb015d0935718dd6ffdfb9eb13b2b8fd0c255c63a2f5cecdb988a3f83df14685.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/03f2711cb5a80e1e94f879bf8a68c25db27f8280f5e1e344425f9ee2a334a829.png)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{4t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/bd4d36cd68896e6360317a0c01c92841423020218436d85ae07eb413463fffa8.png)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-6t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/91c074766b92790a0e78b768b0290d7723617d6ed30e51e842501cad63e58a44.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166469 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/id #uuid "d4296f42-4f86-5ef8-813e-261afa85b829" :question/math-academy-id "e-21597" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/25d4f77a3f596202229581e06d88a9e3bdd84dc6b8b7e14b6f46b3b3d2f125d2.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/343f6f32ac21a1bf06b86174734dd9ebba6ddb7598cbe83b4ade59f70e8e8b26.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/cb0f32e55af289452f1429ddfe3214a46501c55f3a61deaf10b4f736e47dca7f.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/85fdb4fc83a6f2b44178be2e97a0e7985d97350deb4e0fb123e513a80090906a.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/3f984a493e976bd522455395defcb4c1562d61ca56e19fa27e2272832ce6011c.png)\nmust approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 < 0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/44f82894493c95c38039bc1e6a842bb7226571e9818e3c10c50d33627464ea37.png)\nmust also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 < 0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies $\\lambda_1 < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166472 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "4926ad20-fc6b-5a7d-80ee-aa39421c174d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21607" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nInterpret the stability of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the equilibrium point (the origin) for the given decoupled system of linear differential equations." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/7f53fdda091242e4fee1edd70a7e96c649eff8bac93fcdc9fed6da38ec5c7523.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/3b9909cba711a2394b8dbcda4b9d72ffbd9d37bd24b90506ac22b44c4cd398c4.png)\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=2$ with the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/1ea46da3e9697ee89d1112dfec53457657271c391a28a6c55267da70d38157c4.png) The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/9725d696549426d16093b7cd0e9bb188589472ed9ff81520c19a07ab296fb96d.png)\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=4$ with the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/85e8d745e2b504770815bb92ad1a151f422c941874f33840593836e78eeaacde.png) The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/2f67b02cda725daa0d0b994f3f4952065793e53ddc32260b14310f5158876f4f.png)\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{an unstable node (source)}}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/2b79201d15fe5d2a9896cdee09c0474b97bb933d75fcf478c6fdb37cb4d16742.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166474 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "74560a13-4a23-553d-8c8d-6c040f03a957" :question/math-academy-id "e-21633" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/470210fb9509e87c3e51a990bac7483e5862d2f37afdce9823279838e8391592.png)" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/703fb7adc0ca076e42aecc781d4d0dc58eb5f4fec03db4d18bf5a36f7055a349.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/7210186106fc202e3b9a35ffa32b06b09493ce28c191a89c2824a35a547febff.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/f292220b15ff9b0d695cb8b9f2349cc275557fd2b77c32c147e25365ab0f7e47.png)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/5671487738e750f488ad61852f17fb4b6b14cf4050fee66338000d4939914ce6.png)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/651cc55c8b96c402fc00140c06736493e71276666ce4eb1ba74c2294600a1b89.png)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/222dcd944fc89cd0f07970cddf5c50aa167c7f2d25b1f7305779c3dbb0a4a6af.png)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/08ad54b92f62513fb88f12f7bd6da358263e46673d686e0982a1e8044b8ab131.png)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/c5b1aa6ccde39c56b503e2d59a56e2ac8058b08495567f4e2cb40f6a056faedd.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t) = c_1e^{-t}$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{-4t}$ provided that $c_1,c_2 \\neq 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/6c3af2fe68c4bcddbbfd9f13eb84e73014511374c0adc570084f9c92773d31e0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1a41a94-3f78-5fa2-9e87-3c6deb59b975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-331400-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164172} {:answer/id #uuid "a74ccf79-9f4c-51eb-a1b7-aea588079220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-331400-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164173} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f3e703-b154-56ed-812c-db5670969fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-331400-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164174} {:answer/id #uuid "228ba660-34dc-54a5-9c16-eeb1e40d2620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-331400-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164175} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb21ea5-fce1-5855-91d4-8b5da1820a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-331400-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a74ccf79-9f4c-51eb-a1b7-aea588079220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-331400-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164173} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a9fae8e-1db8-5094-a016-0a7168074473" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e5bc024-e1c3-5b73-b155-51e51d92843a" :question/math-academy-id "q-331400" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b72c5263-e813-5760-8a2a-50b09bc507d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332271-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164222} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6b34b7-dfea-50a3-890f-e54e4149c613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332271-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164223} {:answer/id #uuid "c29dc0c9-1402-5fd4-b851-8518a0b25125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332271-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164224} {:answer/id #uuid "06d6331c-9a2b-52e5-bb3c-347be2606275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332271-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164225} {:answer/id #uuid "343c4d7e-644c-5d10-a4a2-908ebbdfe211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332271-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "343c4d7e-644c-5d10-a4a2-908ebbdfe211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332271-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164226} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec9fc6a7-ffbc-5dbd-80ac-d8da2a7e7785" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f707130-0164-506c-911a-bb075d503cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-332271" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/f54544342b11a60ec784160f582b5bdd03a28abee01767a732bef6ac03d9ec62.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{2t})^{′}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}}{2{c}_{1}{e}^{2t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2{c}_{1}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b74ba240-bb6b-54c0-988b-18b9df6de434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332579-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164302} {:answer/id #uuid "e6060709-8fbe-552f-915c-10112e4cf7c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332579-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164303} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3c6800-bc3b-5732-9d76-7074a938d93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332579-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164304} {:answer/id #uuid "11d461ba-548c-5fb8-b18c-7d3d3d4bac7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332579-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164305} {:answer/id #uuid "4f069b5f-95d1-59fb-a6aa-93ebe10fd85b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332579-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b3c6800-bc3b-5732-9d76-7074a938d93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/q-332579-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164304} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef60a38-ade1-584a-977d-fc44e4218ff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7525f92-11a2-54b3-8c7c-6d2f543e65d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332579" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/3784f65f97466e17d857a66ca17e69173de72e9608111c839e989cfff3fe10e0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361252 :knowledge-point/_quest