{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n\\\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/3facfdf59a92a9202d834cd77d14e009c4ec69c5f1b7bd5f12bd17ea1bbc338b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "474eda0d-e16d-55dc-9139-b54cced621ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/01d93cb2e68123b061b057ce30b94bcce44401b2d0fc0c566b3c33478c07fb92.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361268} {:answer/id #uuid "a83fe770-4cff-50ea-89da-f1e71c969ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/27c6bf8d11d3de50beac351f88104df58cac31053eb6ecfc3a35ce77ccd8600b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361269} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1545d8-decf-5210-97f5-a3b220a13d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/7c483bbf926df156b7f85fbedcbb6ab18571ff4f544416cd8ea554b70b59a589.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361270} {:answer/id #uuid "5271f5f2-87ec-548c-a55d-2833d9167aca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/389d3cfc92ece3a63300767f92c34c11dccec981ac43f3f66a2ced02906f8a73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361271} {:answer/id #uuid "0a948d25-808b-5f6c-85ba-b78e21e1d5f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a948d25-808b-5f6c-85ba-b78e21e1d5f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361272} :answer-field/id #uuid "463f8b7e-d330-5602-8f06-624ea4a29211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f7b6c7b-9bbd-58e1-9f4f-ab6d8faeb7f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332683" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-7t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9a3cd4d-872c-5dbe-a775-d669a739a4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361275} {:answer/id #uuid "100b1e97-9643-56fe-a716-3b6c71289202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361276} {:answer/id #uuid "c16ac482-ceb0-54ae-8cc3-cf5efc1a89c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361277} {:answer/id #uuid "556058e0-c209-5ddd-8f75-bd3fd5168102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361278} {:answer/id #uuid "e59027e3-c272-5545-8c5a-97ef943cdb4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "100b1e97-9643-56fe-a716-3b6c71289202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361276} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c8e1d3c-3117-5622-9513-219fc1a16376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fb2564e-076f-58a0-9985-d4e54cdfc878" :question/math-academy-id "q-332692" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/aaeba63f6237121fe226eb30e5378d3e93129bb2782b9c8acb8d73ac719676b3.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "690d61f8-5d34-5956-a15a-8297bf0930bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361282} {:answer/id #uuid "ad87ead6-428a-55fc-af4a-723f096df232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361283} {:answer/id #uuid "ff651e57-c288-5c57-9150-293291e91717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361284} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c9edbc-a42d-58a2-8ad5-56047043fc14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361285} {:answer/id #uuid "870de7e7-1c11-5fc8-828f-69b8e264eeff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff651e57-c288-5c57-9150-293291e91717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361284} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccd3d9ee-c22f-552d-bcae-c867ab056a17" :answer-field/key "se