1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9e0825-5c33-5792-aff8-4bed0337d680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/9fb1f3c545855e7bdfe591574a9069e69bdcc0cc750e5fc4f5aa3eccfdc7ea5a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361309} {:answer/id #uuid "0284ebed-cd7c-560f-ab97-db2904fb4c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/eceeabfeb428687bb9f630fe3578937c3fd4fb910605dc25daf56ad5ce18a68a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361310} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa90dfd-8d3e-5295-926d-2840eb8b6f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361311} {:answer/id #uuid "b2717e8c-9634-59d8-89ca-0556e1b3c415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/9ff104bc583ec025ede6de68f7ea546dea99c914153b8e2325c6f2f9188d0a9b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361312} {:answer/id #uuid "523ee55e-3cca-5570-a3c8-88618f38ccf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/64c0ac27236c4bfae452572e6b5b4c1d6184c45ebac9149ca4fc442d7533bb51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa90dfd-8d3e-5295-926d-2840eb8b6f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361311} :answer-field/id #uuid "33d486d8-424f-575b-8bd1-60eb83cd9f4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a71e1e9-c69e-580f-aa68-2f882f086377" :question/math-academy-id "q-332751" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/96801605aa5d777eb18c13ded1f7b7e59ae74968b210da3b7a26f339a1c9f673.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{7t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{7t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{3t})^{′}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}}{3{c}_{1}{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a584ae8b-b1c8-50fa-8f02-c29ebdd9d58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/4f613e414cdb619a9b09f25a47b7e4cad953440c40d91f876322d7e1cb59b8c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361316} {:answer/id #uuid "11780fdc-41ab-5191-968b-7446ccada44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/635e8687fbccc7612f692ec1a1abaa9802bafe9c3773e4b0c058a18813e7249e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361317} {:answer/id #uuid "148d8f21-24e1-51d6-ad23-a847e1a1806a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/a5ae215022638430d0c89dddd97c01ae7598b6251bd013ef48ca9771029b8cd6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361318} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2dc63f-0cd3-55d5-bdcb-27d2ac53c614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361319} {:answer/id #uuid "358cd386-9b70-5d8e-9f37-7721182d21e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/c9816edf7fb99b759361abc4333f3ed0d3676d2c77db2210367466b66837bca8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f2dc63f-0cd3-55d5-bdcb-27d2ac53c614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361319} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e77d1cb-3c2e-5b2b-9c22-2b27c0142fbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34a4a204-b6f5-559d-8b71-d224e42d358b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333177" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{9t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{9t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/99d6e9894c7117c5684e17702540d0eb469b6e824381cf2263c843f8f55ee8d8.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{9t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{9{c}_{1}{e}^{9t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{9{c}_{1}}{e}^{-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e18a48f2-a3e6-5993-bd4d-8bc1544e7dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/0b2cfbe650613293fe807be4ff7e38fa6f10b2e792dae8f7f4b78f27560f59d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361323} {:answer/id #uuid "78b65467-beeb-50a9-9d8e-90f072a5b5ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/ef7ed85b18002aeec299826750ecc6306c90db8bf2c9bbd9bd8173af0ee9ea41.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361324} {:answer/id #uuid "818b06b6-bdb5-511f-bf8d-ef51c4130f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361325} {:answer/id #uuid "8501b0f6-47e4-54bb-bb05-3c822f5a0bde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/ff9ffa100793101811d8aa6e0e95587ec912ef1e3f64d59fbac617a521479a5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361326} {:answer/id #uuid "54d88cae-6632-5589-9054-d0e2e12ff77b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/b930f98928e3c4af06434c720d953ad0fcee17500113ed9a72492eda4219a7fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "818b06b6-bdb5-511f-bf8d-ef51c4130f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361325} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a10bce-add0-5880-beac-b7920e570aac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91fc904e-97e1-5e34-b331-513c29eb83a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-333184" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/2473117cdca4318187b94374820c0a12576e998e7f631a6e7bf51e56c985be43.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{6t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{6{c}_{1}{e}^{6t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea7c55b8-3874-5d56-a05a-b32c7c1fbfdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361330} {:answer/id #uuid "995de0c4-f622-5377-ae9f-ea063bea83b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/a725947c39aba9b8fb866ef66f6ec7d18986a74cb22973f86bab5e2f45816e68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361331} {:answer/id #uuid "277a5490-6f2d-56bb-a279-e29f7399eb66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/b829471223fd7bc2bd7a6c1183729f6eda34260822edc7c9cb8b6e86a7a55e6e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361332} {:answer/id #uuid "f02986ff-c6f1-5c25-9d12-71e202f215e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/e7a9385b508c7bb1591501fd8bc830faa78ebc3a2f2ad8279ecfe88328ce8bbd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361333} {:answer/id #uuid "e089de5e-fe0b-53ab-8c53-e682261f6d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/71c58730ea546deee365729cab6f1cae3f9aa73409a302067dbbf91fdd85a24d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "995de0c4-f622-5377-ae9f-ea063bea83b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/a725947c39aba9b8fb866ef66f6ec7d18986a74cb22973f86bab5e2f45816e68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361331} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d84b924-2262-5e1f-a86a-a051161a7c6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c54e4f5-9ca4-5f84-ad60-e84341fc1bfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-333192" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/b1efa82f75d22a304e29adc5a791086bb9d9bf43b2f5c97ba8563f43fd13cc4a.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-2t})^{′}}=\\frac{-3{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}}{-2{c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2{c}_{1}}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/a725947c39aba9b8fb866ef66f6ec7d18986a74cb22973f86bab5e2f45816e68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69ffe4b0-5ec6-52ae-a2d5-1b4685e736b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361337} {:answer/id #uuid "a8942790-19b7-51ff-a688-0ce8d433dc5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69ffe4b0-5ec6-52ae-a2d5-1b4685e736b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361337} :answer-field/id #uuid "33d07e7c-af6d-5f25-b763-cabe93a96e16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361336} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d6a1f23-7a93-5b6a-89fe-26cbc9493a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361340} {:answer/id #uuid "127a76f9-d597-507e-9811-49259a9815fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "127a76f9-d597-507e-9811-49259a9815fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361341} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbef879f-f9c1-5f5e-81e8-b1a18b19f450" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361339} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afad6318-d29f-514e-abae-80a768de4f1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361343} {:answer/id #uuid "df5188f4-f05e-5546-bb14-8e5b19419f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361344} {:answer/id #uuid "c1203ee0-0e18-539f-8d9d-c77ff32176d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df5188f4-f05e-5546-bb14-8e5b19419f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361344} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d5198d6-982d-59d3-b12c-97a4b6897156" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d329e132-aa2f-50d8-8bad-87983411294d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333213" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/8c350c21a6206ec73244dd58601b20a816dcb69557a030099870e2c824aba772.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60c60b72-2ecd-5243-8552-91ab5818c1d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361348} {:answer/id #uuid "1938fe30-d429-5d29-8e5a-08d5d6028319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1938fe30-d429-5d29-8e5a-08d5d6028319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361349} :answer-field/id #uuid "0135d4d2-0d50-5b74-ac1e-a4563c03e6da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361347} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185e69a9-d2fc-59aa-868a-86fada81fff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361351} {:answer/id #uuid "eb06c7d4-2f2c-52c7-94a8-681b56992ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "185e69a9-d2fc-59aa-868a-86fada81fff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361351} :answer-field/id #uuid "e932e70e-550f-5e3d-8a53-7bbdc4133d34" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361350} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b89700c2-6f58-5dc4-b78f-3e21caeb4c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361354} {:answer/id #uuid "c2469f82-9e4b-583b-911c-a955b6c6974d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361355} {:answer/id #uuid "408ae78b-d7e4-592a-94a6-51f1329f08a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2469f82-9e4b-583b-911c-a955b6c6974d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361355} :answer-field/id #uuid "12663e43-6002-5381-b18b-3a13529433be" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7471bcb3-e317-52aa-b246-f8dc9f5d93a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-333214" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/6dc8ceb9a1c771f18d6e90d450cd837fe80c6bab7943ab7d397d90eb45754594.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uui